{
  "contract": "llm-attachments",
  "version": 1,
  "description": "Attachments on a single llm.chat call (RFC-0016 in mercury-composable). mercury-python and mercury-nodejs ship this file and the fixture files of tests/fixtures/attachments byte-identical and run every case against their own fake of the Anthropic SDK. For each case the harness makes a new folder with two folders in it, root and outside, sets llm.attachments.root to root (unless the case says root: false, which leaves the key unset) and fills them from files and outside: a relative path maps to the name of a fixture file to copy there, or to {link: target} for a symbolic link with that target, relative to the link's own folder. config adds the keys in force; body is the request; provider is what the fake provider does, kinds as in llm-helper-vectors.json. An expected error (status and message fragments) means the request was refused before any provider call: the harness checks that none was made. Otherwise result is a subset of the reply map and messages is the exact messages parameter of the one provider call, with the last turn's blocks written short: {image: fixture, media_type} is an image block whose base64 source holds that fixture's bytes; {document: fixture} a document block of media type application/pdf whose base64 source holds the fixture's bytes; {document: fixture, pages: [n, ...]} such a block holding a PDF cut from the fixture, which the harness opens with its own PDF library and checks page by page - three-pages.pdf has three pages, page n being 100+n points wide, so the widths of the cut name its pages in order (two libraries do not write the same bytes, so a cut is never compared byte for byte); {text} is a text block. audit_request, where present, is the request column of the turn row the audit store holds for the call once the helper is idle (see llm-audit-vectors.json): references with the source file's size and SHA-256, never a file's bytes.",
  "fixtures": {
    "pixel.png": "a 1x1 PNG",
    "pixel.jpg": "the same pixel as JPEG",
    "pixel.gif": "as GIF",
    "pixel.webp": "as WebP",
    "three-pages.pdf": "three blank pages, 101, 102 and 103 points wide, under a title a cut does not carry",
    "encrypted.pdf": "one page behind a user password",
    "damaged.pdf": "a PDF header and no objects",
    "notes.txt": "plain text"
  },
  "cases": [
